**Theorem 9.2 (Exact corrected complex projection dimension).** For the
literal ideal (1.9)–(1.11),

$$
\boxed{\displaystyle
\dim\overline{\operatorname{pr}_{p,v}V_{\mathbb C}(J_M)}^{\mathrm{Zar}}
=\dim\mathcal B_M=1.}
\tag{9.8}
$$

Equivalently its rational elimination ideal obeys

$$
(0)\ne\Delta_M\ne(1),\qquad
\dim\bigl(\mathbb Q[p,v]/\Delta_M\bigr)=1.
\tag{9.9}
$$

There is no dominating component. The statement permits several curve
components and additional zero-dimensional components; it does not
specify a global base equation or a component factorization.

Proof. The upper bound is Theorem 8.1; the lower bound is
Theorem 9.1. Equation (8.3) proves the equality for the saturated
variety. By the affine elimination theorem its projected closure
is the zero set of the elimination ideal after extension to
$\mathbb C$. Field extension commutes with this contraction and
preserves Krull dimension. Therefore the rational quotient has
dimension one. A zero ideal would give dimension two; a unit
ideal would give an empty zero set. Both are excluded, proving
(9.9). The no-dominance assertion is already included in Theorem
8.1. No expansion of a bivariate eliminant is used in this argument. ∎

